
// Original practice expansion: first foundation chapters.
const foundationQuestionUpdates=[{"chapter":"Motion in a Straight Line","questions":[["A car covers equal distances at 20 m/s and 30 m/s. Its average speed is:",["25 m/s","10 m/s","50 m/s","24 m/s"],3,"For equal distances, average speed is 2v₁v₂/(v₁+v₂)=1200/50=24 m/s."],["A car travels for equal time intervals at 20 m/s and 30 m/s. Its average speed is:",["60 m/s","12 m/s","25 m/s","24 m/s"],2,"Equal time intervals give average speed (20+30)/2=25 m/s."],["A particle has position x=3t²+2t in metres, with t in seconds. Its velocity at t=2 s is:",["8 m/s","14 m/s","16 m/s","12 m/s"],1,"Velocity is dx/dt=6t+2; at 2 s it is 14 m/s."],["For x=4t³ metres with t in seconds, acceleration at t=1 s is:",["24 m/s²","12 m/s²","4 m/s²","8 m/s²"],0,"Differentiate twice: v=12t² and a=24t; at 1 s, a=24 m/s²."],["A particle starts with velocity 4 m/s and acceleration −2 m/s². When does it first come to rest?",["4 s","1 s","8 s","2 s"],3,"Set v=u+at=4−2t=0, giving t=2 s."],["A body starts from rest with constant acceleration 3 m/s². Distance covered during the fourth second is:",["13.5 m","6 m","10.5 m","24 m"],2,"s(4)−s(3)=½×3×(16−9)=10.5 m."],["A stone is dropped from rest through 45 m. Neglect air resistance and use g=10 m/s². Fall time is:",["1.5 s","3 s","4.5 s","9 s"],1,"h=½gt², so t=√(2×45/10)=3 s."],["A ball is thrown vertically upwards at 20 m/s. With g=10 m/s² and no drag, maximum height above launch is:",["20 m","40 m","10 m","200 m"],0,"At the highest point v=0. Height is u²/(2g)=400/20=20 m."],["A ball thrown vertically upwards at 30 m/s returns to its launch height. Use g=10 m/s² and neglect drag. Total time is:",["3 s","9 s","1.5 s","6 s"],3,"Time of flight back to the same height is 2u/g=60/10=6 s."],["Two vehicles move along the same straight road in the same direction at 18 m/s and 12 m/s. Relative speed is:",["15 m/s","216 m/s","6 m/s","30 m/s"],2,"For motion in the same direction, relative speed is the magnitude of the difference: 18−12=6 m/s."],["Two cyclists approach one another along a straight road at 5 m/s and 7 m/s. Their separation decreases at:",["6 m/s","12 m/s","2 m/s","35 m/s"],1,"Approaching speeds add, giving 5+7=12 m/s."],["A 120 m train passes a stationary pole in 8 s at constant speed. Train speed is:",["15 m/s","8 m/s","960 m/s","120 m/s"],0,"The train moves one train length: speed=120/8=15 m/s."],["A 100 m train passes completely over a 200 m bridge at 20 m/s. Time taken is:",["10 s","5 s","20 s","15 s"],3,"The front must travel train length plus bridge length: (100+200)/20=15 s."],["A velocity–time graph is a horizontal line at −3 m/s for 4 s. Displacement during that interval is:",["−3 m","0 m","−12 m","12 m"],2,"Signed area under the graph is (−3)×4=−12 m."],["A particle moves at +2 m/s for 3 s, then −2 m/s for 3 s. Its average speed over 6 s is:",["1 m/s","2 m/s","0 m/s","4 m/s"],1,"Total distance is 6+6=12 m, so average speed is 12/6=2 m/s despite zero displacement."],["A body moving at 10 m/s brakes with constant acceleration −5 m/s². Stopping distance is:",["10 m","20 m","5 m","50 m"],0,"Using v²=u²+2as with v=0 gives 0=100−10s, so s=10 m."],["For constant acceleration, doubling the initial speed while keeping the same braking acceleration magnitude makes stopping distance:",["Twice as large","Half as large","Unchanged","Four times as large"],3,"Stopping distance is u²/(2|a|), so doubling u multiplies distance by four."],["At the highest point of a vertical throw, neglecting drag, which statement is correct?",["Acceleration is upward","Gravity disappears","Velocity is zero but acceleration is downward","Velocity and acceleration are both zero"],2,"Instantaneous velocity vanishes at the turning point, but gravity still causes downward acceleration g."],["A position–time curve becomes progressively steeper in the positive direction. This indicates:",["Negative displacement necessarily","Increasing positive velocity","Constant positive velocity","Zero acceleration necessarily"],1,"The slope dx/dt is velocity. Increasing positive slope means positive velocity is increasing."],["For one-dimensional motion, a negative acceleration necessarily means:",["Acceleration points in the negative chosen direction","The body is slowing down","Velocity is negative","Distance travelled is negative"],0,"The sign gives direction relative to the chosen axis. A body with negative velocity and negative acceleration speeds up."]]},{"chapter":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","questions":[["Using molar mass 32 g/mol, the mass of 0.25 mol O₂ is:",["4 g","32 g","128 g","8 g"],3,"Mass=nM=0.25×32=8 g."],["How many molecules are present in 0.5 mol CO₂? Use N_A=6.022×10²³ mol⁻¹.",["1.2044×10²⁴","0.5","3.011×10²³","6.022×10²³"],2,"Molecule count=nN_A=0.5×6.022×10²³=3.011×10²³."],["Two moles of NH₃ molecules contain how many moles of hydrogen atoms?",["8 mol","6 mol","2 mol","3 mol"],1,"Each NH₃ molecule has three hydrogen atoms; 2×3=6 mol hydrogen atoms."],["Using atomic masses C=12 and O=16, carbon mass percentage in CO₂ is approximately:",["27.3%","72.7%","12%","50%"],0,"Molar mass is 44 g/mol; carbon mass fraction is 12/44. Multiply by 100 to obtain 27.3%."],["A compound contains 40% C, 6.67% H and 53.33% O by mass. Using atomic masses 12, 1 and 16, its empirical formula is:",["C₂H₂O","CHO₂","C₂H₆O","CH₂O"],3,"For 100 g, mole amounts are approximately 3.33, 6.67 and 3.33. Dividing by 3.33 gives C:H:O=1:2:1."],["A compound has empirical formula CH₂O and molar mass 180 g/mol. Its molecular formula is:",["C₃H₆O₃","C₁₂H₂₄O₁₂","C₆H₁₂O₆","CH₂O"],2,"Empirical formula mass is 30 g/mol. The multiplier 180/30=6 gives C₆H₁₂O₆."],["For N₂+3H₂→2NH₃, 2 mol N₂ and 3 mol H₂ react completely as far as possible. The limiting reactant is:",["Neither reactant","H₂","N₂","NH₃"],1,"Compare n/coefficient: N₂ gives 2/1=2, H₂ gives 3/3=1. Hydrogen has the smaller value and limits reaction."],["For N₂+3H₂→2NH₃, 2 mol N₂ and 3 mol H₂ can produce at most:",["2 mol NH₃","4 mol NH₃","3 mol NH₃","6 mol NH₃"],0,"Three moles H₂ react with one mole N₂ to form two moles NH₃; one mole N₂ remains."],["A reaction has theoretical yield 20 g and actual isolated yield 15 g. Percentage yield is:",["133.3%","25%","5%","75%"],3,"Percentage yield=100×actual/theoretical=100×15/20=75%."],["0.2 mol solute is dissolved to make 500 mL solution. Molarity is:",["100 mol/L","2.5 mol/L","0.4 mol/L","0.1 mol/L"],2,"Convert 500 mL to 0.5 L. M=n/V=0.2/0.5=0.4 mol/L."],["0.5 mol solute is dissolved in 250 g solvent. Molality is:",["500 mol/kg","2 mol/kg","0.125 mol/kg","0.5 mol/kg"],1,"Molality uses solvent mass in kilograms: 0.5/0.250=2 mol/kg."],["A mixture contains 2 mol ethanol and 3 mol water. Mole fraction of ethanol is:",["0.4","0.6","2/3","2"],0,"Total amount is 5 mol; ethanol mole fraction is 2/5=0.4."],["100 mL of 2 mol/L solution is diluted to 500 mL without reaction. Final molarity is:",["10 mol/L","1 mol/L","2 mol/L","0.4 mol/L"],3,"M₁V₁=M₂V₂, so M₂=2×100/500=0.4 mol/L."],["10 g solute mixed with 90 g solvent gives a solution with solute mass percentage:",["90%","1%","10%","11.1%"],2,"Solution mass=10+90=100 g. Solute mass percentage=100×10/100=10%."],["At 273.15 K and 1 atm, an ideal gas has molar volume approximately 22.4 L/mol. Volume of 0.5 mol is:",["5.6 L","11.2 L","22.4 L","44.8 L"],1,"At these stated conditions, V=nV_m=0.5×22.4=11.2 L."],["At the same temperature and pressure, 1 L ideal H₂ and 1 L ideal O₂ contain:",["Equal numbers of molecules","Equal masses","Equal numbers of oxygen atoms","Equal mass densities"],0,"Avogadro's law gives equal molecule counts for equal ideal-gas volumes at the same T and P, despite differing molar masses."],["The number of significant figures in 0.00450 is:",["2","5","6","3"],3,"Leading zeros are placeholders; 4, 5 and the final decimal zero are significant."],["To the correct decimal place, 12.11+0.3 equals:",["12","12.410","12.4","12.41"],2,"Addition is rounded to the least precise decimal place. The 0.3 term has one decimal place, so 12.41 rounds to 12.4."],["Using atomic masses Ca=40, C=12 and O=16, molar mass of CaCO₃ is:",["116 g/mol","100 g/mol","68 g/mol","84 g/mol"],1,"Add 40+12+3×16=100 g/mol."],["A pure sample contains 3.011×10²³ helium atoms. Using N_A=6.022×10²³ mol⁻¹, its amount is:",["0.5 mol","2 mol","1 mol","3.011 mol"],0,"n=N/N_A=(3.011×10²³)/(6.022×10²³)=0.5 mol."]]},{"chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","questions":[["In the fluid mosaic model, the main framework of the plasma membrane is:",["A cellulose sheet","A DNA double helix","A peptidoglycan bilayer","A phospholipid bilayer"],3,"Amphipathic phospholipids form a bilayer with hydrophilic heads towards water and hydrophobic tails inward; proteins are embedded or associated."],["The hydrophobic fatty-acid tails of membrane phospholipids mainly point:",["Only towards cytoplasm","Towards ribosomal RNA","Towards the bilayer interior","Only towards extracellular water"],2,"Nonpolar tails avoid water and face inward, while polar heads face aqueous environments on both sides."],["Transport against a concentration gradient generally requires:",["A cellulose pump","Energy input and transport machinery","Only simple diffusion","No membrane protein under any condition"],1,"Active transport uses energy directly or through a coupled gradient to move substances against their gradients."],["Facilitated diffusion differs from simple diffusion because it:",["Uses membrane transport proteins","Always consumes ATP directly","Moves only against a gradient","Occurs only across cell walls"],0,"Facilitated diffusion is passive movement down a gradient through channels or carriers; it does not require direct ATP hydrolysis."],["Most bacterial ribosomes are described as:",["80S","60S","40S","70S"],3,"Bacterial ribosomes are 70S particles formed from 50S and 30S subunits. Sedimentation coefficients are not arithmetically additive."],["Typical eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes contain which subunits?",["40S and 30S","70S and 80S","60S and 40S","50S and 30S"],2,"Eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S, consisting of 60S and 40S subunits."],["Which cell structure is not enclosed by a membrane?",["Golgi cisterna","Ribosome","Lysosome","Mitochondrion"],1,"Ribosomes are ribonucleoprotein complexes without a bounding membrane; the other listed structures are membrane bounded."],["Smooth endoplasmic reticulum is especially associated with:",["Lipid synthesis","Chromosome segregation exclusively","Formation of bacterial peptidoglycan","Storage of genetic information"],0,"Smooth ER participates in lipid synthesis and, in specialised cells, detoxification and calcium handling."],["A secreted protein is commonly processed along which route?",["Chloroplast → nucleus → cell wall","Nucleolus → mitochondrion → ribosome","Lysosome → DNA → centriole","Rough ER → Golgi → secretory vesicle"],3,"Proteins entering the secretory pathway are synthesised into the rough ER, processed in the Golgi and transported in vesicles."],["The inward folds of the mitochondrial inner membrane are called:",["Cisternae of Golgi","Nuclear pores","Cristae","Grana"],2,"Cristae increase the inner membrane surface area that supports respiratory electron transport and ATP synthesis."],["Stacks of thylakoids in chloroplasts are called:",["Nucleoli","Grana","Cristae","Centrioles"],1,"A granum is a stack of thylakoids; grana is the plural. Thylakoid membranes house photosynthetic light-reaction machinery."],["The fluid region of a chloroplast surrounding thylakoids is the:",["Stroma","Matrix of mitochondrion","Periplasm of bacteria","Nucleoplasm"],0,"The chloroplast stroma contains enzymes for carbon fixation as well as chloroplast DNA and ribosomes."],["The large central vacuole of a mature plant cell is bounded by the:",["Plasmalemma only","Nuclear envelope","Middle lamella","Tonoplast"],3,"The tonoplast is the vacuolar membrane and controls exchange between vacuole contents and cytoplasm."],["The middle lamella between adjacent plant cells is chiefly rich in:",["Peptidoglycan","Glycogen","Calcium pectate","Chitin"],2,"The pectin-rich middle lamella, commonly described as calcium pectate, helps cement neighbouring plant cells together."],["Channels connecting the cytoplasm of neighbouring plant cells are:",["Ribosomal subunits","Plasmodesmata","Cristae","Desmosomes"],1,"Plasmodesmata traverse cell walls and provide cytoplasmic connections for communication and transport."],["The nuclear envelope of a typical eukaryotic cell consists of:",["Two membranes with nuclear pores","A single cellulose layer","No membranes","A peptidoglycan shell"],0,"A double membrane separates nuclear contents from cytoplasm; pores regulate nucleocytoplasmic exchange."],["A typical motile eukaryotic cilium has an axonemal arrangement of:",["Nine peripheral triplets and no central pair","Nine single microtubules only","Two peripheral doublets and nine central singlets","Nine peripheral microtubule doublets and two central singlets"],3,"The conventional motile axoneme has a 9+2 arrangement. Some specialised cilia differ from this typical pattern."],["A typical centriole shows which microtubule arrangement?",["Two central triplets only","Eight peripheral single microtubules","Nine peripheral triplets with no central pair","Nine peripheral doublets plus two central singlets"],2,"A centriole is typically organised as nine peripheral triplets, conventionally described as 9+0."],["Which group typically has a cell wall containing chitin?",["Most bacteria","Fungi","Animals","Flowering plants"],1,"Fungal cell walls characteristically contain chitin. Plant walls chiefly contain cellulose; bacterial walls commonly contain peptidoglycan."],["A mature mammalian red blood cell differs from most animal cells because it lacks:",["A nucleus and mitochondria","A plasma membrane","Haemoglobin","Cytoplasm"],0,"During maturation, mammalian erythrocytes lose their nucleus and mitochondria but retain plasma membrane, cytoplasm and haemoglobin."]]}];
foundationQuestionUpdates.forEach(u=>{const l=LESSONS.find(l=>l.chapter===u.chapter);if(!l)throw new Error("Missing chapter: "+u.chapter);l.quiz.push(...u.questions);l.body=l.body.replace("ten-question chapter quiz","chapter quiz");});

// Second original practice expansion.
const secondQuestionUpdates=[{"chapter":"Laws of Motion","questions":[["A 5 kg block lies on a horizontal surface with μs=0.5. Use g=10 m/s². A horizontal force of 10 N is applied and the block remains at rest. Friction magnitude is:",["25 N","50 N","0 N","10 N"],3,"The maximum static friction is 0.5×50=25 N, but static friction supplies only the 10 N needed for equilibrium."],["A sliding 5 kg block is on a horizontal surface with μk=0.2. Use g=10 m/s². Kinetic friction magnitude is:",["2 N","25 N","10 N","50 N"],2,"With no other vertical forces, N=mg=50 N. Kinetic friction is μkN=0.2×50=10 N."],["A 2 kg block on a frictionless horizontal surface is pulled by 10 N horizontally. Acceleration is:",["10 m/s²","5 m/s²","20 m/s²","0.2 m/s²"],1,"Net horizontal force is 10 N, so a=F/m=10/2=5 m/s²."],["Two horizontal forces, 18 N rightward and 6 N leftward, act on a 3 kg block. Its acceleration is:",["4 m/s² rightward","8 m/s² rightward","4 m/s² leftward","6 m/s² rightward"],0,"Choose right positive. Net force=18−6=12 N and acceleration=12/3=4 m/s² rightward."],["A 60 kg person stands on a scale in a lift accelerating upwards at 2 m/s². Use g=10 m/s². The scale's normal force is:",["600 N","480 N","120 N","720 N"],3,"N−mg=ma, giving N=m(g+a)=60×12=720 N."],["A 60 kg person stands on a scale in a lift accelerating downwards at 2 m/s². Use g=10 m/s². The scale's normal force is:",["600 N","120 N","480 N","720 N"],2,"With upward positive, N−mg=−ma; N=m(g−a)=60×8=480 N."],["A lift and its passenger undergo ideal free fall. The passenger's apparent weight is:",["Equal to mass in kilograms","Zero","Equal to mg","Twice mg"],1,"Both accelerate at g downward. No support force is required, so the normal reaction and apparent weight vanish."],["A 4 kg block rests on a smooth plane inclined at 30° to horizontal. Use g=10 m/s². The downslope gravitational component is:",["20 N","40 N","20√3 N","10 N"],0,"The component parallel to the plane is mg sin30°=4×10×½=20 N."],["A block slides down a smooth plane inclined at 30°. Use g=10 m/s². Its acceleration down the plane is:",["10 m/s²","5√3 m/s²","0 m/s²","5 m/s²"],3,"On a frictionless incline, a=g sinθ=10×½=5 m/s², independent of mass."],["A 4 kg block rests on a plane inclined at 30° with no other normal force. Use g=10 m/s². The normal reaction is:",["40 N","10√3 N","20√3 N","20 N"],2,"Normal balance gives N=mg cos30°=40×√3/2=20√3 N."],["Masses 3 kg and 2 kg hang over an ideal massless frictionless pulley with a light string. Use g=10 m/s². Acceleration magnitude is:",["1 m/s²","2 m/s²","10 m/s²","5 m/s²"],1,"For an ideal Atwood machine, a=(m₁−m₂)g/(m₁+m₂)=(3−2)×10/5=2 m/s²."],["For an ideal Atwood machine with masses 3 kg and 2 kg and g=10 m/s², string tension is:",["24 N","30 N","20 N","50 N"],0,"Acceleration is 2 m/s². For the ascending 2 kg mass, T−20=2×2, so T=24 N."],["A 2 kg and a 3 kg block touch on a smooth horizontal table. A 20 N force pushes the 2 kg block towards the 3 kg block. Contact force on the 3 kg block is:",["20 N","8 N","4 N","12 N"],3,"The common acceleration is 20/(2+3)=4 m/s². The contact force accelerating the 3 kg block is 3×4=12 N."],["A 0.2 kg ball moving at +10 m/s rebounds with velocity −10 m/s. Change in momentum is:",["0 kg·m/s","−2 kg·m/s","−4 kg·m/s","+4 kg·m/s"],2,"Δp=m(v−u)=0.2×(−10−10)=−4 kg·m/s. The sign indicates the chosen negative direction."],["A constant net force of 8 N changes momentum by 24 kg·m/s. Duration of the force is:",["16 s","3 s","192 s","0.33 s"],1,"Impulse FΔt=Δp, so Δt=24/8=3 s."],["A force–time graph is a triangle with base 4 s and peak force 10 N, all positive. Impulse is:",["20 N·s","40 N·s","10 N·s","2.5 N·s"],0,"Impulse equals area under the force–time graph: ½×4×10=20 N·s."],["A car travels around a level circular road. The force providing horizontal centripetal acceleration is ordinarily:",["Weight alone","Normal reaction alone","A separate outward force","Static friction from the road"],3,"Weight and normal reaction balance vertically. Tyre-road static friction provides the inward horizontal force when tyres do not slide."],["If speed doubles at fixed mass and circular-path radius, the required centripetal force becomes:",["Half as large","Unchanged","Four times as large","Twice as large"],2,"F=mv²/r; doubling v multiplies v² and hence required inward force by four."],["A frictionless banked circular road has r=20 m and tanθ=0.5. Use g=10 m/s². Suitable speed is:",["100 m/s","10 m/s","20 m/s","5 m/s"],1,"Frictionless banking requires tanθ=v²/(rg). Thus v=√(20×10×0.5)=10 m/s."],["Inertia of a body is quantitatively measured by its:",["Mass","Speed","Acceleration","Applied force"],0,"Mass measures resistance to changes in velocity. More mass requires more net force for a given acceleration."]]},{"chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","questions":[["The usual molecular shape of methane, CH₄, is:",["Square planar","Trigonal planar","Linear","Tetrahedral"],3,"Four bonding electron domains around carbon and no lone pairs give tetrahedral geometry with ideal angles near 109.5°."],["BF₃ is commonly described as having which geometry around boron?",["Tetrahedral","Bent","Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal"],2,"Three bonding domains and no lone pair on boron give trigonal planar geometry, about 120°."],["An isolated gaseous BeCl₂ molecule has which usual shape?",["Trigonal pyramidal","Linear","Bent","Tetrahedral"],1,"The isolated molecule has two bonding domains around Be and no lone pairs in the elementary VSEPR model, giving 180° geometry. Solid-state structure differs."],["In a water molecule, the number of lone pairs on the central oxygen in its standard Lewis structure is:",["Two","One","Zero","Three"],0,"Oxygen has six valence electrons. Two O–H bonds leave four nonbonding electrons, or two lone pairs."],["In NH₃, the nitrogen has how many bonding pairs and lone pairs in the usual Lewis structure?",["Four bonding pairs and no lone pair","Two bonding pairs and two lone pairs","One bonding pair and three lone pairs","Three bonding pairs and one lone pair"],3,"Nitrogen's five valence electrons support three N–H bonds and one nonbonding pair."],["The usual hybridisation assigned to carbon in methane is:",["sp","dsp²","sp³","sp²"],2,"Four equivalent sigma bonds in the elementary bonding model are described using four sp³ hybrid orbitals."],["Each carbon in ethene is usually assigned which hybridisation?",["sp³d","sp²","sp³","sp"],1,"Each carbon has three sigma-bond directions and one unhybridised p orbital used for the pi bond, corresponding to sp²."],["Each carbon in ethyne is usually assigned which hybridisation?",["sp","sp²","sp³","sp³d²"],0,"Each carbon has two sigma-bond directions and two unhybridised p orbitals forming two pi bonds, giving sp hybridisation."],["A carbon–carbon triple bond contains:",["Three sigma bonds","Two sigma and one pi bond","Three pi bonds","One sigma and two pi bonds"],3,"One head-on overlap gives the sigma bond, while two sideways overlaps give the two pi bonds."],["The total number of sigma and pi bonds in ethene, C₂H₄, is:",["Six sigma and zero pi","Three sigma and three pi","Five sigma and one pi","Four sigma and two pi"],2,"There are four C–H sigma bonds and one C–C sigma bond; the double bond also contributes one pi bond."],["The total number of sigma and pi bonds in ethyne, C₂H₂, is:",["Four sigma and one pi","Three sigma and two pi","Two sigma and three pi","Five sigma and zero pi"],1,"Two C–H sigma bonds plus one C–C sigma bond give three sigma bonds. The triple bond supplies two pi bonds."],["In VSEPR theory, the usual ordering of repulsions is:",["Lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair > bond pair–bond pair","Bond pair–bond pair > lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair","All repulsions are identical","Lone pairs cause no repulsion"],0,"Lone pairs occupy more angular space near the central atom than bonding pairs, giving stronger repulsions in the elementary model."],["For comparable carbon–carbon bonds, the usual bond-length ordering is:",["Triple > double > single","Double > single > triple","All lengths are equal","Single > double > triple"],3,"Greater bond order generally shortens the bond, so a single C–C bond is longer than a double bond, which is longer than a triple bond."],["In molecular-orbital theory, the bond order of H₂ is:",["2","0.5","1","0"],2,"Two electrons occupy the bonding sigma orbital and none the antibonding orbital: (2−0)/2=1."],["In the elementary molecular-orbital model, He₂ has bond order:",["0.5","0","1","2"],1,"Its four electrons fill two bonding and two antibonding positions, so (2−2)/2=0; the model predicts no ordinary stable covalent He₂ molecule."],["The molecular-orbital bond order of N₂ is:",["3","2","1","0"],0,"The ground-state molecular-orbital electron count gives eight bonding and two antibonding valence electrons: (8−2)/2=3."],["Removing one electron from an antibonding orbital of O₂ to form O₂⁺ changes its bond order from 2 to:",["1.5","1","3","2.5"],3,"Reducing the antibonding electron count by one increases (N_b−N_a)/2 by one half."],["Which molecule is polar under its usual molecular geometry?",["BF₃","CH₄","NH₃","CO₂"],2,"Trigonal pyramidal NH₃ has a nonzero net dipole. Symmetric CO₂, BF₃ and CH₄ have cancelling bond dipoles."],["The formal charge on nitrogen in the usual NH₄⁺ Lewis structure is:",["+2","+1","−1","0"],1,"Formal charge=valence electrons−nonbonding electrons−half bonding electrons=5−0−4=+1."],["Resonance contributors of a molecule differ principally in:",["Electron placement while nuclear positions remain fixed","Positions of the nuclei","Total atom count","Overall molecular charge"],0,"Resonance contributors represent different allowed electron arrangements for the same nuclear framework. The real molecule is a resonance hybrid, not rapidly switching structures."]]},{"chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","questions":[["For an Aa×Aa cross with ordinary segregation, the probability of an aa offspring is:",["1/2","3/4","1","1/4"],3,"Each parent passes allele a with probability 1/2; the probability of receiving a from both is 1/2×1/2=1/4."],["For an AA×aa cross, the expected offspring genotype is:",["All aa","Half AA and half aa","All Aa","All AA"],2,"The AA parent contributes A to every offspring and the aa parent contributes a, producing heterozygous Aa offspring."],["Under independent assortment, an AaBb individual produces how many distinct allele combinations in gametes?",["One","Four","Two","Eight"],1,"The two heterozygous independently assorting loci give 2²=4 gamete combinations: AB, Ab, aB and ab."],["Under independent assortment, an AaBbCc individual can form how many distinct allele combinations in gametes?",["Eight","Six","Three","Sixteen"],0,"For three independently assorting heterozygous loci, combinations=2³=8."],["For AaBb×AaBb with independent assortment, the probability of aabb offspring is:",["1/4","3/16","9/16","1/16"],3,"At each locus the recessive homozygote probability is 1/4. Multiply independent probabilities: 1/4×1/4=1/16."],["For AaBb×aabb with independent assortment, the probability of AaBb offspring is:",["1/2","3/4","1/4","1/16"],2,"The AaBb parent makes AB gametes with probability 1/4; the aabb parent makes only ab gametes, so AaBb occurs with probability 1/4."],["An individual is heterozygous at four independently assorting loci. Number of allele combinations in gametes is:",["32","16","8","4"],1,"The number of combinations is 2ⁿ with n=4 heterozygous independent loci, giving 16."],["In snapdragons with incomplete dominance, red RR×white rr produces F₁ flowers that are:",["Pink Rr","All red RR","All white rr","Half red and half white"],0,"All offspring are Rr and show the intermediate pink phenotype in the standard snapdragon example."],["Crossing pink Rr snapdragons with white rr under incomplete dominance gives which expected phenotype ratio?",["3 pink : 1 white","All pink","1 red : 2 pink : 1 white","1 pink : 1 white"],3,"The Rr parent supplies R or r equally and the rr parent only r, giving half Rr pink and half rr white."],["In the ABO blood-group system, alleles Iᴬ and Iᴮ are:",["Identical alleles","Always lethal together","Codominant to each other","Both recessive to i"],2,"IᴬIᴮ individuals express both A and B antigens. Both alleles are dominant to i in the standard ABO model."],["A parent with genotype Iᴬi and a parent with genotype Iᴮi can have children of which ABO phenotypes?",["Only O","A, B, AB and O","Only A and B","Only AB"],1,"Combining Iᴬ or i with Iᴮ or i gives IᴬIᴮ, Iᴬi, Iᴮi and ii, corresponding to AB, A, B and O."],["In the usual ABO model, a blood-group O individual has genotype:",["ii","IᴬIᴮ","Iᴬi","Iᴮi"],0,"O results from homozygous ii; Iᴬ and Iᴮ encode the standard A and B antigen-producing activities."],["An IᴬIᴮ parent and an ii parent can have children with which usual ABO blood groups?",["AB or O","Only AB","A, B, AB or O","A or B"],3,"The AB parent contributes Iᴬ or Iᴮ and the O parent only i, producing Iᴬi or Iᴮi offspring."],["In human XY sex determination, a typical sperm carries:",["Only an X chromosome","Neither X nor Y chromosome","An X chromosome or a Y chromosome","Both X and Y chromosomes"],2,"Normal haploid sperm carry either X or Y. Typical ova carry X, so the sperm determines XX or XY at fertilisation."],["A carrier mother XᴴXʰ and unaffected father XᴴY have sons. Assuming equal segregation, the probability that a son has the recessive X-linked trait is:",["1","1/2","1/4","0"],1,"Conditional on being a son, the child receives Y from the father and one maternal X. Half receive Xʰ and express the recessive trait."],["For an X-linked recessive trait, an affected father XʰY and homozygous unaffected mother XᴴXᴴ have daughters who are:",["All carriers XᴴXʰ","All affected XʰXʰ","All homozygous unaffected XᴴXᴴ","Half affected and half unaffected"],0,"Every daughter receives Xʰ from the father and Xᴴ from the mother, becoming an unaffected carrier under the stated recessive model."],["Two genes very close together on the same chromosome ordinarily show:",["Exactly 100% recombination","No inheritance","Independent assortment in every meiosis","Low recombination frequency"],3,"Close loci have fewer crossovers between them and therefore lower recombinant frequency than widely separated loci."],["A genetic cross produces 40 recombinant offspring among 200 total offspring. Recombination frequency is:",["5%","80%","20%","40%"],2,"Recombination frequency=100×40/200=20%. For short intervals this can approximate map distance in centimorgans."],["The law of segregation concerns the separation of:",["All linked loci in every meiosis","The two alleles at a locus during gamete formation","All proteins from DNA","Dominant phenotypes from recessive phenotypes after birth"],1,"A diploid individual's two alleles at a locus separate during gamete formation so each typical gamete receives one allele."],["Sickle-cell anaemia illustrates pleiotropy because a change in one gene can:",["Affect multiple phenotypic features","Create only one phenotype under all conditions","Prevent allele segregation","Make every trait polygenic"],0,"Pleiotropy means a single gene influences multiple features. The haemoglobin gene variant can affect red-cell shape and several physiological outcomes."]]}];
